    "summary": "The remains of three Pakistani sailors killed in an attack off Somalia's coast were repatriated via Addis Ababa, along with four injured crew members. The sailors were aboard the MT Honour 25 when the incident occurred.",
    "content": "The bodies of three Pakistani sailors who were killed in an attack on the vessel MT Honour 25 off the coast of Somalia were flown from Addis Ababa to Karachi on Saturday, according to diplomatic sources. The deceased were identified as Rafi Ullah Khan, Syed Kashif Umer, and Mahmood Ahmad Ansari. Four other crew members who were injured in the same incident—Usman Ghani, Aqeel Khan, Ameen bin Shams, and Yasir Khan—were also on the Ethiopian Airlines flight ET 694.\n\nThe transfer was overseen by Pakistan's ambassadors to Ethiopia and Djibouti, who attended a ceremony in Addis Ababa to bid farewell to the bodies and the injured sailors. The Pakistani government has stated that it is coordinating with relevant authorities to ensure the safe return of its citizens. The attack on the MT Honour 25 is part of a series of maritime security incidents in the waters off Somalia, a region long plagued by piracy and armed robbery at sea.\n\nSomali waters have seen renewed attacks on commercial shipping in recent months, raising concerns about the safety of international crews. The MT Honour 25, a cargo vessel, was reportedly targeted while navigating the Gulf of Aden or nearby waters. The exact circumstances of the attack remain unclear, but Somali authorities have previously pledged to crack down on piracy. The injured sailors are reported to be in stable condition and are expected to receive further medical care upon arrival in Pakistan.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this incident underscores the persistent security challenges in their homeland's waters. Many in the diaspora have family members involved in maritime trade or rely on stable shipping routes for goods and remittances. The deaths of foreign sailors also highlight the human cost of instability, which can deter international investment and exacerbate economic hardships. Diaspora communities often act as bridges between Somalia and the world, and incidents like this can strain those connections.\n\nPakistani authorities have not released further details about the attack or potential suspects. The repatriation process involved coordination between Pakistani embassies in Ethiopia and Djibouti, as well as Ethiopian Airlines. The Somali government has yet to issue a statement on the incident. The injured sailors are expected to be reunited with their families in Pakistan, while the deceased will be buried according to local customs.\n\nThis event serves as a reminder of the risks faced by seafarers in the region and the importance of international cooperation to ensure maritime security. For the Somali diaspora, it reinforces the need for continued engagement in rebuilding and stabilizing their country, even as they navigate the complexities of life abroad. The repatriation of the sailors' remains marks a somber conclusion to a tragedy that has reverberated across borders.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/04/meydadka-saddex-badmaax-oo-soomaaliya-lagu-dilay-oo-loo-qaaday-pakistan/",
